This peer constructs, from a finite list of basic neighbourhood indices, the finitely generated subsystem it determines and then a domain whose elements encode all subsystems of the original neighbourhood system.

<!-- formalization -->

**Definition (`combineSub`).** Given a list and two of its sublists, their combination retains exactly those entries of the original list that occur in at least one sublist.

**Theorem (`combineSub_sublist`).** The combined list is a sublist of the original list.

*Proof.* It is obtained by filtering the original list. ∎

**Theorem (`mem_combineSub`).** An index belongs to the combined list exactly when it belongs to the original list and to at least one of the two selected sublists.

*Proof.* This is the membership criterion for the defining filter. ∎

**Theorem (`interFrom_combineSub_eq`).** If both selected lists are sublists of the same list, intersecting the basic neighbourhoods selected by their combination equals the intersection of the two separately generated intersections.

*Proof.* Membership on either side says that the point lies in the master neighbourhood and in every basic neighbourhood selected by either sublist. ∎

**Definition (`finGen`).** The finite system generated by a list consists of those neighbourhoods of the ambient system that are intersections of the master neighbourhood with the basic neighbourhoods indexed by some sublist.

**Theorem (`finGen_mono`).** Enlarging the list of generators enlarges its finite generated system.

*Proof.* Every sublist of the smaller list is also a sublist of the larger one. ∎

**Theorem (`finGen_sub_mem`).** Every member of a finite generated system is an ambient neighbourhood.

*Proof.* Ambient membership is part of the defining witness. ∎

**Theorem (`finGen_sub_master`).** Every member of a finite generated system is contained in the ambient master neighbourhood.

*Proof.* Every ambient neighbourhood is contained in the master neighbourhood. ∎

**Theorem (`master_mem_finGen`).** The master neighbourhood belongs to every finite generated system.

*Proof.* Use the empty sublist, whose generated intersection is the master neighbourhood. ∎

**Theorem (`principal_mem_finGen`).** If an index occurs in the generating list, its basic neighbourhood belongs to the finite generated system.

*Proof.* Use the singleton sublist containing that index. ∎

**Definition (`finGenSys`).** The finite generated system is made into a neighbourhood system with the ambient master neighbourhood and with the finite intersections generated by sublists as its neighbourhoods.

**Theorem (`mem_finGenSys`).** Membership in the finite generated neighbourhood system is exactly membership in the corresponding finite generated family.

*Proof.* This is the definition of its neighbourhood predicate. ∎

**Theorem (`finGenSys_subsystem`).** Every finite generated neighbourhood system is a subsystem of the ambient system.

*Proof.* Its neighbourhoods are ambient neighbourhoods, and combining sublists witnesses closure under every ambient intersection that exists. ∎

**Theorem (`interFrom_cons_eq`).** The intersection generated by a list beginning with \(j\) is the intersection of the \(j\)-th basic neighbourhood with the intersection generated by the remaining list.

*Proof.* Separate the head condition from the conditions indexed by the tail. ∎

**Theorem (`finGen_le_iff_forall_mem`).** For a subsystem of the ambient system, the finite system generated by a list is contained in that subsystem exactly when every basic neighbourhood indexed by the list belongs to the subsystem.

*Proof.* One direction uses the singleton-generated members. For the converse, induction on the list and subsystem closure under ambient intersections add one generator at a time. ∎

**Theorem (`finGen_append_le_iff`).** The finite system generated by the concatenation of two lists is contained in a subsystem exactly when each of the two separately generated systems is contained in it.

*Proof.* Apply the generator criterion and split universal membership over concatenation. ∎

**Definition (`nbhdGen`).** The neighbourhood generated by a list is the set of lists whose finite generated systems contain the one generated by the given list.

**Theorem (`mem_nbhdGen`).** A list belongs to the neighbourhood generated by another list exactly when its finite generated system contains that of the first list.

*Proof.* This is the defining membership condition. ∎

**Theorem (`nbhdGen_antitone`).** Inclusion of finite generated systems reverses inclusion of their generated neighbourhoods.

*Proof.* Compose the two inclusions of finite systems. ∎

**Theorem (`self_mem_nbhdGen`).** Every list belongs to the neighbourhood it generates.

*Proof.* Its finite generated system contains itself. ∎

**Theorem (`nbhdGen_inter`).** The intersection of the neighbourhoods generated by two lists is the neighbourhood generated by their concatenation.

*Proof.* Membership means containing both finite generated systems, which by the concatenation criterion is equivalent to containing the finite system generated by the concatenated list. ∎

**Definition (`SubD`).** The subsystem domain has finite lists as tokens and takes the generated neighbourhoods as its neighbourhoods; the neighbourhood generated by the empty list is its master neighbourhood.

**Theorem (`finGen_eq_of_nbhdGen_eq`).** Equal generated neighbourhoods have equal finite generated systems.

*Proof.* Each generating list belongs to its own neighbourhood, so equality supplies both inclusions between the finite generated systems. ∎

**Theorem (`nbhdGen_eq_iff`).** Two generated neighbourhoods are equal exactly when their finite generated systems are equal.

*Proof.* The forward implication is the preceding result, and the reverse implication follows directly from the containment definition. ∎

**Theorem (`mem_SubD`).** A set of lists is a neighbourhood of the subsystem domain exactly when it is generated by some finite list.

*Proof.* This is the neighbourhood predicate of the subsystem domain. ∎

**Theorem (`SubD_master`).** The master neighbourhood of the subsystem domain is generated by the empty list.

*Proof.* This is its defining master neighbourhood. ∎

**Theorem (`exists_common_witness`).** If an element of the subsystem domain confirms, separately, witnesses that generate every basic neighbourhood indexed by a finite list, then one confirmed list jointly generates all those basic neighbourhoods.

*Proof.* Induct on the list and concatenate witnesses; filter closure gives confirmation of their intersection, while monotonicity carries the required generators into the concatenation. ∎

**Theorem (`interFrom_append_eq`).** The intersection generated by a concatenated list equals the intersection of the intersections generated by its two parts.

*Proof.* Membership splits into the conditions indexed by the two component lists. ∎

**Definition (`ofSubsystem`).** A subsystem determines an element of the subsystem domain by confirming a generated neighbourhood exactly when the subsystem contains every indexed basic neighbourhood in its generating list.

**Theorem (`mem_ofSubsystem_nbhdGen`).** The element associated with a subsystem confirms the neighbourhood generated by a list exactly when the subsystem contains every basic neighbourhood indexed by that list.

*Proof.* Equality of generated neighbourhoods is converted to equality of finite generated systems, after which the finite-generator criterion applies. ∎

**Definition (`toSubsystemSys`).** An element of the subsystem domain determines a neighbourhood system whose neighbourhoods are members of finite systems generated by lists confirmed by that element.

**Theorem (`toSubsystemSys_subsystem`).** The neighbourhood system determined by an element of the subsystem domain is a subsystem of the ambient system.

*Proof.* Its members are ambient neighbourhoods, and two confirmed witnesses can be concatenated to witness closure under ambient intersection. ∎

**Theorem (`ofSubsystem_toSubsystem`).** Passing from an element to its represented subsystem and back returns the original element.

*Proof.* A confirmed list plainly supplies its indexed generators; conversely, finitely many separate witnesses can be combined into one common confirmed witness and then enlarged to the desired neighbourhood. ∎

**Theorem (`mem_nbhdGen_iff`).** An element confirms the neighbourhood generated by a list exactly when its represented subsystem contains every basic neighbourhood indexed by that list.

*Proof.* Replace the element by the element reconstructed from its represented subsystem and apply the membership characterization above. ∎

**Definition (`toSubsystem`).** Each element of the subsystem domain is sent to the ambient subsystem it represents.

**Theorem (`toSubsystem_ofSubsystem_mem`).** Reconstructing a subsystem from its associated element preserves its neighbourhoods exactly.

*Proof.* A generated witness yields subsystem membership by the finite-generator criterion; conversely, a basic index for the given neighbourhood supplies a singleton witness. ∎

**Theorem (`toSubsystem_ofSubsystem`).** Passing from a subsystem to its associated element and back returns the original subsystem.

*Proof.* Their neighbourhood predicates agree by the preceding theorem, and their master neighbourhoods are the same. ∎
